    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the radius of the circle defined by the equation \\(x^2 - 6x + y^2 + 2y + 6 = 0\\), we start by completing the square for both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms.\n\nFirst consider the \\(x\\) terms:\n\\[\nx^2 - 6x\n\\]\nTo complete the square, take half the coefficient of \\(x\\) (which is \\(-6\\)), square it, and add and subtract the result inside the equation. Half of \\(-6\\) is \\(-3\\), and its square is \\(9\\):\n\\[\nx^2 - 6x = (x - 3)^2 - 9\n\\]\n\nNext, consider the \\(y\\) terms:\n\\[\ny^2 + 2y\n\\]\nTo complete the square, take half the coefficient of \\(y\\) (which is \\(2\\)), square it, and add and subtract the result inside the equation. Half of \\(2\\) is \\(1\\), and its square is \\(1\\):\n\\[\ny^2 + 2y = (y + 1)^2 - 1\n\\]\n\nNow substitute these completed squares back into the original equation:\n\\[\nx^2 - 6x + y^2 + 2y + 6 = 0\n\\]\nbecomes\n\\[\n(x - 3)^2 - 9 + (y + 1)^2 - 1 + 6 = 0\n\\]\n\nCombine the constants on the left side:\n\\[\n(x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 - 4 = 0\n\\]\n\nMove the \\(-4\\) to the right side of the equation:\n\\[\n(x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 4\n\\]\n\nThis is the standard form of the circle equation:\n\\[\n(x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2\n\\]\nwhere \\((h, k)\\) represents the center and \\(r\\) is the radius.\n\nComparing with our equation:\n\\[\n(x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 4\n\\]\n\nFrom this, we see that:\n\\[\n(h, k) = (3, -1) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad r^2 = 4\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides to find \\(r\\):\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{4} = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the radius of the circle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
